                  "content": "SELECTED: candidate_68\n\nCONFIDENCE: HIGH\n\nEXPLANATION:\nThe fix commit modifies the fall-through switch statement in KeychainDatabase.onUpgrade(), removing a spurious extra `case 8:` label (merging it into `case 7:`) and renumbering every subsequent case (9->8, 10->9, 11->10, 12->11, 13->12, 14->13, 15->14). This is the \"ridiculous database migration bug\": at some point an extra, unnecessary case label was inserted into the fall-through chain, causing every migration step added afterward to be off by one relative to the actual DATABASE_VERSION it corresponds to.\n\ncandidate_68 is where this exact problematic pattern was first introduced. In this commit:\n- `DATABASE_VERSION` is bumped from 7 to 8.\n- A new `CREATE_API_APPS_ALLOWED_KEYS` table/migration is added.\n- Crucially, the migration code is added as TWO separate case labels instead of one:\n    case 7:\n        // consolidate\n    case 8:\n        // new table for allowed key ids in API\n        try { db.execSQL(CREATE_API_APPS_ALLOWED_KEYS); } ...\n\nThis is the precise code block (word-for-word, including the \"// consolidate\" comment and the API_APPS_ALLOWED_KEYS try/catch) that the fix commit later collapses into a single `case 7:` before renumbering all later cases down by one. No other candidate introduces or contains this \"case 7: // consolidate\" followed by a distinct \"case 8:\" pattern \u2014 it originates exactly here. Because the switch statement relies purely on fall-through with no breaks, this extra case label silently introduced an extra \"slot\" in the migration sequence that every subsequently-added case (9, 10, 11, ...) inherited, compounding the numbering bug until it was corrected in the fix commit.\n"
                }
